// Psalms 107–150 (Book V) at three reading levels (see psalms.js).
export default [
  { // 107
    s: 'Let the redeemed thank God for rescuing them from every kind of trouble.',
    m: 'The psalm calls the redeemed of the LORD to give thanks for His steadfast love. It describes four groups in distress: wanderers in the desert, prisoners in darkness, the sick, and sailors in a storm. Each time they cry to the LORD, He saves them, and they are called to thank Him for His wonderful works.',
    d: '"Oh give thanks to the LORD, for he is good, for his steadfast love endures forever! Let the redeemed of the LORD say so," the psalm begins, as God gathers His people from east and west, north and south. It then paints four scenes: some wandered in desert wastes, hungry and thirsty; some sat in darkness as prisoners because they rebelled against God\'s words; some were fools sick through their sinful ways, drawing near to death; and some went down to the sea in ships, where a storm made them reel like drunkards at their wits\' end. In every case "they cried to the LORD in their trouble, and he delivered them from their distress," leading the wanderers to a city, breaking the prisoners\' chains, sending His word to heal the sick, and stilling the storm to a whisper, with the refrain, "Let them thank the LORD for his steadfast love, for his wondrous works to the children of man!" The psalm ends by describing how God turns deserts into pools and raises the needy, and urges, "Whoever is wise, let him attend to these things; let them consider the steadfast love of the LORD."',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 108
    s: 'David\'s steadfast heart praises God and trusts Him for victory.',
    m: 'David combines parts of Psalms 57 and 60 into a new song. He declares that his heart is steadfast and he will awaken the dawn with praise, for God\'s steadfast love is great above the heavens. He asks God to give help against the enemy, since human help is worthless, but with God they will do valiantly.',
    d: 'This psalm of David joins the praise of Psalm 57:7–11 with the prayer of Psalm 60:5–12. "My heart is steadfast, O God! I will sing and make melody with all my being!" he begins, resolving to awake the dawn and praise God among the nations, "for your steadfast love is great above the heavens." He then prays for deliverance, recalling God\'s promise that Shechem, Gilead, Ephraim and Judah are His, and that He will triumph over Moab, Edom and Philistia. He asks who will bring him to Edom, and closes, "Oh grant us help against the foe, for vain is the salvation of man! With God we shall do valiantly; it is he who will tread down our foes."',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 109
    s: 'David asks God to judge a slanderer who repaid love with hatred.',
    m: 'David asks God not to keep silent while wicked and deceitful people attack him with lies. In return for his love they accuse him, though he gives himself to prayer. He calls down judgment on his accuser, then pleads for God to help him for His name\'s sake, so people will know it is God\'s hand that saves him.',
    d: '"Be not silent, O God of my praise!" David cries, because wicked mouths speak against him with lying tongues and attack him without cause, repaying evil for good and hatred for his love, "but I give myself to prayer." In an intense imprecatory section, he prays that his accuser be judged, that his days be few and another take his office, and that his children and possessions suffer, a passage Peter applies to Judas in Acts 1:20. Then David turns to his own need: "But you, O GOD my Lord, deal on my behalf for your name\'s sake," for he is poor and needy, his heart is stricken, and he is gone like a shadow, his knees weak from fasting and mocked by others. He asks God to save him according to His steadfast love so they may know "this is your hand," and he vows to give great thanks, "for he stands at the right hand of the needy one, to save him from those who condemn his soul to death."',
    p: ['david', 'judas'],
  },
  { // 110
    s: 'The LORD tells David\'s Lord to sit at His right hand as King and Priest.',
    m: 'David hears the LORD say to his Lord, "Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies your footstool." This King will rule from Zion and is declared "a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek." Jesus used this psalm to show that the Messiah is greater than David, and it is the Old Testament passage most quoted in the New Testament.',
    d: 'David writes, "The LORD says to my Lord: \'Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies your footstool,\'" describing God enthroning a King whom David himself calls Lord, a puzzle Jesus used to show that the Messiah is not merely David\'s son but his Lord (Matthew 22:41–46). The LORD sends out His mighty scepter from Zion, and His people offer themselves freely on the day of His power, in holy garments, with the dew of youth. "The LORD has sworn and will not change his mind, \'You are a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek,\'" uniting king and priest in one person, which Hebrews 5–7 explains at length as fulfilled in Jesus. The Lord at God\'s right hand will shatter kings on the day of His wrath and judge among the nations, drinking from the brook by the way and lifting up His head, and this psalm is quoted or alluded to in the New Testament more than any other.',
    p: ['david', 'jesus', 'abraham'],
  },
  { // 111
    s: 'Great are the works of the LORD; the fear of the LORD is wisdom\'s beginning.',
    m: 'This acrostic psalm praises the LORD with the whole heart for His great, glorious and gracious works. He provides food for those who fear Him, remembers His covenant forever, and sent redemption to His people. It ends declaring that the fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom.',
    d: '"Praise the LORD! I will give thanks to the LORD with my whole heart, in the company of the upright, in the congregation," the psalm begins, an acrostic with each line starting with the next Hebrew letter. "Great are the works of the LORD, studied by all who delight in them," and His work is full of splendor and majesty, His righteousness endures forever, and He has caused His wondrous works to be remembered, for He is gracious and merciful. He provides food for those who fear Him, remembers His covenant forever, gave His people the inheritance of the nations, and His precepts are trustworthy, established forever. "He sent redemption to his people; he has commanded his covenant forever. Holy and awesome is his name! The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom; all those who practice it have a good understanding."',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 112
    s: 'Blessed is the one who fears the LORD and is generous.',
    m: 'This companion to Psalm 111 describes the blessings of the person who fears the LORD and delights in His commands. Such a person is gracious, merciful and generous, lending and dealing justly. He is not afraid of bad news, for his heart is firm and trusts the LORD, and he gives freely to the poor.',
    d: '"Praise the LORD! Blessed is the man who fears the LORD, who greatly delights in his commandments!" the acrostic begins, echoing Psalm 111 by showing how God\'s character is reflected in those who fear Him. His offspring will be mighty, wealth and riches are in his house, and his righteousness endures forever, for "light dawns in the darkness for the upright; he is gracious, merciful, and righteous." It is well with the man who deals generously and lends and conducts his affairs with justice, and he will never be moved but will be remembered forever. "He is not afraid of bad news; his heart is firm, trusting in the LORD," and because he has distributed freely and given to the poor, his righteousness endures forever, a verse Paul quotes about generous giving (2 Corinthians 9:9), while the wicked see it and are angry.',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 113
    s: 'The LORD on high stoops down to lift up the poor.',
    m: 'The servants of the LORD are called to praise His name from the rising of the sun to its setting. He is high above all nations, yet He looks far down on the heavens and the earth. He raises the poor from the dust and gives the barren woman a home as a joyful mother of children.',
    d: 'This psalm opens the "Egyptian Hallel" (Psalms 113–118), sung at Passover: "Praise the LORD! Praise, O servants of the LORD, praise the name of the LORD!" His name is to be blessed from this time forth and forevermore, and praised from the rising of the sun to its setting. "Who is like the LORD our God, who is seated on high, who looks far down on the heavens and the earth?" The answer is shown in His mercy: He raises the poor from the dust and lifts the needy from the ash heap to seat them with princes, and gives the barren woman a home, making her the joyous mother of children, as with Sarah, Hannah and others.',
    p: ['sarah', 'hannah'],
  },
  { // 114
    s: 'When Israel left Egypt, the sea fled and the mountains skipped.',
    m: 'This short poem celebrates the Exodus, when Judah became God\'s sanctuary and Israel His dominion. The sea fled, the Jordan turned back, and the mountains skipped like rams before the presence of the Lord. The earth is called to tremble before the God who turns rock into a pool of water.',
    d: '"When Israel went out from Egypt, the house of Jacob from a people of strange language, Judah became his sanctuary, Israel his dominion," the psalm begins. In lively poetry, "the sea looked and fled; Jordan turned back. The mountains skipped like rams, the hills like lambs." The psalmist playfully asks the sea, the Jordan and the mountains what was wrong with them that they fled and skipped. The answer: "Tremble, O earth, at the presence of the Lord, at the presence of the God of Jacob, who turns the rock into a pool of water, the flint into a spring of water."',
    p: ['jacob'],
  },
  { // 115
    s: 'Not to us, but to your name give glory; idols are powerless.',
    m: 'The psalmist prays, "Not to us, O LORD, but to your name give glory," because of God\'s steadfast love and faithfulness. Unlike lifeless idols with mouths that cannot speak and eyes that cannot see, God is in the heavens and does whatever He pleases. Israel, the priests and all who fear the LORD are called to trust Him, for He will bless them.',
    d: '"Not to us, O LORD, not to us, but to your name give glory, for the sake of your steadfast love and your faithfulness!" the psalm begins, answering the nations who ask, "Where is their God?" with, "Our God is in the heavens; he does all that he pleases." Their idols are silver and gold, made by human hands, with mouths that do not speak, eyes that do not see, ears that do not hear, and feet that do not walk, and "those who make them become like them; so do all who trust in them." Israel, the house of Aaron and all who fear the LORD are called to trust in Him, for He is their help and shield, and He will bless both small and great. The heavens are the LORD\'s, but the earth He has given to mankind, and since the dead do not praise Him, "we will bless the LORD from this time forth and forevermore. Praise the LORD!"',
    p: ['aaron'],
  },
  { // 116
    s: 'I love the LORD because He heard my cry and saved me from death.',
    m: 'The psalmist loves the LORD because He heard his voice when the snares of death surrounded him. God is gracious, righteous and merciful, and He delivered his soul from death and his eyes from tears. In gratitude, he will lift up the cup of salvation, call on the LORD\'s name, and pay his vows before all God\'s people.',
    d: '"I love the LORD, because he has heard my voice and my pleas for mercy," the psalmist says, describing how the snares of death encompassed him and he called, "O LORD, I pray, deliver my soul!" "Gracious is the LORD, and righteous; our God is merciful," he declares, telling his soul to return to its rest, for God has delivered him from death, his eyes from tears, and his feet from stumbling, so he will walk before the LORD in the land of the living. He asks, "What shall I render to the LORD for all his benefits to me?" answering that he will lift up the cup of salvation, call on His name and pay his vows in the presence of all His people. "Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of his saints," he says, and as God\'s servant whose bonds have been loosed, he offers a sacrifice of thanksgiving in the courts of the LORD\'s house.',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 117
    s: 'All nations, praise the LORD for His great love.',
    m: 'The shortest psalm calls all nations and peoples to praise the LORD. The reason is that His steadfast love toward us is great. His faithfulness endures forever.',
    d: 'In just two verses, the shortest chapter of the Bible calls, "Praise the LORD, all nations! Extol him, all peoples!" The reason is simple and profound: "For great is his steadfast love toward us, and the faithfulness of the LORD endures forever. Praise the LORD!" Though it speaks of God\'s love "toward us," meaning Israel, it invites every nation to praise Him. Paul quotes it in Romans 15:11 to show that God always intended Gentiles to glorify Him for His mercy.',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 118
    s: 'His love endures forever; the rejected stone has become the cornerstone.',
    m: 'The psalm repeatedly declares that God\'s steadfast love endures forever and recounts how the LORD rescued the writer when surrounded by enemies. He enters the gates of righteousness to give thanks, declaring that the stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone. The crowds sang its words when Jesus entered Jerusalem: "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the LORD!"',
    d: '"Oh give thanks to the LORD, for he is good; for his steadfast love endures forever!" the psalm begins, calling Israel, the house of Aaron and all who fear the LORD to say it too. Out of distress the writer called on the LORD, who answered and set him free, so he declares, "The LORD is on my side; I will not fear. What can man do to me?" and that it is better to take refuge in the LORD than to trust in man or princes. Though all nations surrounded him like bees, in the name of the LORD he cut them off, and he says, "I shall not die, but I shall live, and recount the deeds of the LORD," then asks to enter the gates of righteousness to give thanks. "The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone. This is the LORD\'s doing; it is marvelous in our eyes. This is the day that the LORD has made; let us rejoice and be glad in it," words Jesus applied to Himself, and the crowds at His triumphal entry shouted, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the LORD!" (Matthew 21:9, 42).',
    p: ['jesus', 'aaron'],
  },
  { // 119
    s: 'A long love song to God\'s word, a lamp to our feet.',
    m: 'The longest chapter in the Bible is an acrostic of 176 verses, with eight verses for each letter of the Hebrew alphabet, all celebrating God\'s word. The psalmist delights in God\'s law, hides it in his heart, and finds it sweeter than honey and more precious than gold. God\'s word is a lamp to his feet and a light to his path, and he prays for understanding and faithfulness to obey it.',
    d: 'Psalm 119 is arranged in twenty-two stanzas of eight verses, each stanza beginning with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet, and nearly every verse refers to God\'s word using terms such as law, testimonies, precepts, statutes, commandments, rules and promises. It begins, "Blessed are those whose way is blameless, who walk in the law of the LORD," and asks, "How can a young man keep his way pure? By guarding it according to your word," adding, "I have stored up your word in my heart, that I might not sin against you." Throughout, the psalmist faces affliction, scorn and persecution from the proud, yet says, "It is good for me that I was afflicted, that I might learn your statutes," declares, "Oh how I love your law! It is my meditation all the day," and finds God\'s words "sweeter than honey to my mouth." Famous lines include "Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path" and "The sum of your word is truth," and the psalm ends humbly, "I have gone astray like a lost sheep; seek your servant, for I do not forget your commandments."',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 120
    s: 'A song of ascents: longing for peace among people who love war.',
    m: 'This first of fifteen "Songs of Ascents," sung by pilgrims going up to Jerusalem, begins with a cry for deliverance from lying lips and a deceitful tongue. The psalmist feels like a stranger living among violent peoples. He is for peace, but when he speaks, they are for war.',
    d: 'Psalms 120–134 are titled "A Song of Ascents," likely sung by pilgrims traveling up to Jerusalem for the feasts, and this first one begins far from Zion. "In my distress I called to the LORD, and he answered me," the writer says, asking, "Deliver me, O LORD, from lying lips, from a deceitful tongue." He warns the deceitful tongue that it will receive a warrior\'s sharp arrows and glowing coals of the broom tree. "Woe to me, that I sojourn in Meshech, that I dwell among the tents of Kedar!" he laments, naming distant, warlike peoples, and closes, "I am for peace, but when I speak, they are for war," a fitting reason to set out for God\'s city of peace.',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 121
    s: 'My help comes from the LORD, who never sleeps.',
    m: 'The pilgrim lifts his eyes to the hills and asks where his help comes from. His help comes from the LORD, maker of heaven and earth, who will not let his foot slip and never slumbers or sleeps. The LORD keeps him from all evil and will guard his going out and coming in forevermore.',
    d: '"I lift up my eyes to the hills. From where does my help come?" the pilgrim asks, perhaps looking toward the hills around Jerusalem or the dangerous hills along the road. "My help comes from the LORD, who made heaven and earth," he answers, and a second voice assures him that God will not let his foot be moved, for "he who keeps Israel will neither slumber nor sleep." The word "keep" repeats throughout: the LORD is your keeper and your shade on your right hand, so the sun will not strike by day nor the moon by night. "The LORD will keep you from all evil; he will keep your life. The LORD will keep your going out and your coming in from this time forth and forevermore."',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 122
    s: 'I was glad to go to the house of the LORD; pray for Jerusalem\'s peace.',
    m: 'David rejoices when people say, "Let us go to the house of the LORD," and finally stands within Jerusalem\'s gates. The tribes go up there to give thanks, and the thrones of justice are set there. He calls everyone to pray for the peace of Jerusalem and seek its good for the sake of God\'s house.',
    d: '"I was glad when they said to me, \'Let us go to the house of the LORD!\'" David writes, and now the pilgrims\' feet are standing within Jerusalem\'s gates. He describes Jerusalem as a city bound firmly together, to which the tribes go up as decreed to give thanks to the name of the LORD, and where the thrones for judgment of the house of David are set. "Pray for the peace of Jerusalem! May they be secure who love you!" he urges, asking for peace within its walls and security within its towers. For the sake of his brothers and companions and for the sake of the house of the LORD, he says, "I will seek your good."',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 123
    s: 'Like servants watching their master, we look to God for mercy.',
    m: 'The psalmist lifts his eyes to God enthroned in heaven. Like servants watching their master\'s hand, the people look to the LORD until He shows them mercy. They ask for mercy because they have endured too much contempt from the proud.',
    d: '"To you I lift up my eyes, O you who are enthroned in the heavens!" the psalm begins, as the pilgrim looks above even the hills to God Himself. "Behold, as the eyes of servants look to the hand of their master, as the eyes of a maidservant to the hand of her mistress, so our eyes look to the LORD our God, till he has mercy upon us." The people pray, "Have mercy upon us, O LORD, have mercy upon us, for we have had more than enough of contempt." Their souls have had more than enough of the scorn of those at ease and the contempt of the proud, so they wait patiently and humbly for God\'s help.',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 124
    s: 'If the LORD had not been on our side, we would have been swallowed up.',
    m: 'David asks Israel to imagine what would have happened if the LORD had not been on their side when enemies attacked. They would have been swallowed alive or swept away by a flood. But God rescued them like a bird escaping a broken snare, for their help is in the name of the LORD, who made heaven and earth.',
    d: '"If it had not been the LORD who was on our side—let Israel now say—if it had not been the LORD who was on our side when people rose up against us," David begins, prompting the congregation to respond. Their enemies\' anger would have swallowed them alive, the flood would have swept them away, and the raging waters would have gone over them. "Blessed be the LORD, who has not given us as prey to their teeth!" they sing. "We have escaped like a bird from the snare of the fowlers; the snare is broken, and we have escaped! Our help is in the name of the LORD, who made heaven and earth."',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 125
    s: 'Those who trust the LORD are as secure as Mount Zion.',
    m: 'Those who trust in the LORD are like Mount Zion, which cannot be moved but stands forever. As the mountains surround Jerusalem, the LORD surrounds His people. The psalmist prays for God to do good to the upright and leave evildoers to their own crooked ways, and ends, "Peace be upon Israel!"',
    d: '"Those who trust in the LORD are like Mount Zion, which cannot be moved, but abides forever," the pilgrims sing as they approach Jerusalem. "As the mountains surround Jerusalem, so the LORD surrounds his people, from this time forth and forevermore." The scepter of wickedness will not rest on the land allotted to the righteous, lest the righteous stretch out their hands to do wrong. The psalmist prays, "Do good, O LORD, to those who are good, and to those who are upright in their hearts!" while those who turn aside to crooked ways will be led away with evildoers, and he concludes, "Peace be upon Israel!"',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 126
    s: 'Those who sow in tears will reap with shouts of joy.',
    m: 'When the LORD restored Zion\'s fortunes, it felt like a dream, and the people\'s mouths were filled with laughter and joy. Even the nations said the LORD had done great things for them. Now they pray for God to restore them again, trusting that those who sow in tears will reap with shouts of joy.',
    d: '"When the LORD restored the fortunes of Zion, we were like those who dream," the psalm begins, perhaps remembering the return from exile, when "our mouth was filled with laughter, and our tongue with shouts of joy." The nations said, "The LORD has done great things for them," and the people agree, "The LORD has done great things for us; we are glad." Yet more restoration is needed, so they pray, "Restore our fortunes, O LORD, like streams in the Negeb," the dry southern streambeds that suddenly flood with rain. "Those who sow in tears shall reap with shouts of joy! He who goes out weeping, bearing the seed for sowing, shall come home with shouts of joy, bringing his sheaves with him."',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 127
    s: 'Unless the LORD builds the house, the builders labor in vain.',
    m: 'In this psalm of Solomon, all human effort is useless without the LORD: unless He builds the house or guards the city, the work is in vain. It is pointless to work anxiously from early morning to late at night, for God gives sleep to His beloved. Children are a heritage and reward from the LORD, like arrows in a warrior\'s hand.',
    d: 'Attributed to Solomon, the builder of the temple, the psalm declares, "Unless the LORD builds the house, those who build it labor in vain. Unless the LORD watches over the city, the watchman stays awake in vain." It is vain to rise up early and go late to rest, eating the bread of anxious toil, "for he gives to his beloved sleep." The "house" may also mean a family, as the psalm turns to children: "Behold, children are a heritage from the LORD, the fruit of the womb a reward." Like arrows in the hand of a warrior are the children of one\'s youth, and blessed is the man who fills his quiver with them, for he will not be put to shame when he speaks with his enemies in the gate.',
    p: ['solomon'],
  },
  { // 128
    s: 'The one who fears the LORD is blessed at work and at home.',
    m: 'Blessed is everyone who fears the LORD and walks in His ways. Such a person will enjoy the fruit of his labor, his wife will be like a fruitful vine, and his children like olive shoots around his table. The psalm ends with a blessing from Zion for prosperity, long life and peace on Israel.',
    d: '"Blessed is everyone who fears the LORD, who walks in his ways!" the psalm begins, describing God\'s ordinary, daily blessings. "You shall eat the fruit of the labor of your hands; you shall be blessed, and it shall be well with you." The home is pictured beautifully: "Your wife will be like a fruitful vine within your house; your children will be like olive shoots around your table." The psalm ends with a priestly blessing: "The LORD bless you from Zion! May you see the prosperity of Jerusalem all the days of your life! May you see your children\'s children! Peace be upon Israel!"',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 129
    s: 'Israel has been afflicted often, but the LORD has cut the cords of the wicked.',
    m: 'Israel recounts how enemies have greatly afflicted them since their youth, plowing on their backs like a field. Yet their enemies have not prevailed, because the righteous LORD has cut the cords of the wicked. The psalmist prays that those who hate Zion will wither like grass on a rooftop.',
    d: '"Greatly have they afflicted me from my youth—let Israel now say," the psalm begins, looking back on centuries of oppression since Egypt. "Yet they have not prevailed against me," though "the plowers plowed upon my back; they made long their furrows," a vivid image of whip marks. "The LORD is righteous; he has cut the cords of the wicked," setting His people free. The psalmist prays that all who hate Zion will be put to shame and be like grass on housetops, which withers before it grows and that no reaper gathers, so that no one passing by will say, "The blessing of the LORD be upon you!"',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 130
    s: 'Out of the depths I cry; with the LORD there is forgiveness.',
    m: 'From the depths of distress, the psalmist cries to the LORD for mercy. If God kept a record of sins, no one could stand, but with Him there is forgiveness. He waits for the LORD more than watchmen wait for the morning, and he calls Israel to hope in the LORD, who will redeem them from all their iniquities.',
    d: '"Out of the depths I cry to you, O LORD! O Lord, hear my voice!" the psalmist begins, one of the penitential psalms. "If you, O LORD, should mark iniquities, O Lord, who could stand? But with you there is forgiveness, that you may be feared," showing that grace leads to reverence rather than carelessness. "I wait for the LORD, my soul waits, and in his word I hope; my soul waits for the Lord more than watchmen for the morning, more than watchmen for the morning." He turns to the whole nation: "O Israel, hope in the LORD! For with the LORD there is steadfast love, and with him is plentiful redemption. And he will redeem Israel from all his iniquities."',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 131
    s: 'Like a weaned child, David\'s soul is calm and quiet in God.',
    m: 'David says his heart is not proud and he does not concern himself with things too great for him. Instead, he has calmed and quieted his soul like a weaned child with its mother. He calls Israel to hope in the LORD now and forever.',
    d: 'In this brief psalm of David, he says, "O LORD, my heart is not lifted up; my eyes are not raised too high; I do not occupy myself with things too great and too marvelous for me." He is content to trust God rather than demand answers to everything. "But I have calmed and quieted my soul, like a weaned child with its mother; like a weaned child is my soul within me," a picture of a child who no longer frets for milk but rests contentedly with its mother. He closes, "O Israel, hope in the LORD from this time forth and forevermore."',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 132
    s: 'God chose Zion and promised David a lasting dynasty.',
    m: 'The psalm remembers David\'s hardships and his vow not to rest until he found a dwelling place for the LORD. The people call God to arise and go to His resting place with the ark, and to remember His anointed. God swore to David that his sons would sit on his throne, and He has chosen Zion as His home forever, where He will make a horn sprout for David.',
    d: 'The pilgrims ask the LORD to remember David and all the hardships he endured, especially his oath that he would not sleep until he found a place for the LORD, a dwelling for the Mighty One of Jacob. They recall how the ark was found and brought to Jerusalem and sing, "Arise, O LORD, and go to your resting place, you and the ark of your might," praying for the priests and saints and for God\'s anointed. "The LORD swore to David a sure oath from which he will not turn back: \'One of the sons of your body I will set on your throne,\'" if his sons keep the covenant. God has chosen Zion, saying, "This is my resting place forever; here I will dwell," promising to bless her provisions, satisfy her poor, clothe her priests with salvation, and "there I will make a horn to sprout for David; I have prepared a lamp for my anointed," pointing ahead to the Messiah.',
    p: ['david', 'jesus'],
  },
  { // 133
    s: 'How good it is when God\'s people live together in unity.',
    m: 'David declares how good and pleasant it is when brothers live together in unity. Such unity is like precious oil running down Aaron\'s beard and robes. It is like the dew of Mount Hermon falling on Zion, for there the LORD commands His blessing, life forevermore.',
    d: '"Behold, how good and pleasant it is when brothers dwell in unity!" David writes, perhaps as pilgrims from all the tribes gather in Jerusalem. Unity is "like the precious oil on the head, running down on the beard, on the beard of Aaron, running down on the collar of his robes," recalling the abundant anointing oil that consecrated the high priest. It is also "like the dew of Hermon, which falls on the mountains of Zion," bringing refreshment and life from the north to the south. "For there the LORD has commanded the blessing, life forevermore."',
    p: ['david', 'aaron'],
  },
  { // 134
    s: 'Servants of the LORD, bless Him in His house by night.',
    m: 'The last song of ascents calls the servants of the LORD who stand in His house by night to bless Him. They are told to lift up their hands to the holy place and bless the LORD. In return, a blessing is spoken over them from the LORD, maker of heaven and earth, who blesses from Zion.',
    d: 'The final Song of Ascents closes the pilgrimage with worship in the temple. "Come, bless the LORD, all you servants of the LORD, who stand by night in the house of the LORD!" it begins, addressing the priests and Levites who kept watch through the night. "Lift up your hands to the holy place and bless the LORD!" Then the blessing returns: "May the LORD bless you from Zion, he who made heaven and earth!"',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 135
    s: 'Praise the LORD, who is greater than all gods and chose Israel.',
    m: 'The servants of the LORD are called to praise His name, for He is good and has chosen Jacob for Himself. The LORD is greater than all gods, does whatever He pleases in heaven and earth, and struck down Egypt and the kings of Canaan to give Israel the land. Idols are lifeless and those who trust them become like them, so all Israel should bless the LORD.',
    d: '"Praise the LORD! Praise the name of the LORD, give praise, O servants of the LORD," the psalm begins, calling those in the courts of God\'s house to sing, "for the LORD is good," and He has chosen Jacob as His own possession. "For I know that the LORD is great, and that our Lord is above all gods. Whatever the LORD pleases, he does," whether making clouds rise, sending lightning and rain, or bringing the wind from His storehouses. He struck down the firstborn of Egypt, sent signs against Pharaoh, and struck down great nations and kings such as Sihon and Og to give their land as a heritage to Israel, for His name endures forever and He will vindicate His people. Echoing Psalm 115, it mocks the idols of the nations, made of silver and gold, with mouths that cannot speak and eyes that cannot see, and calls the house of Israel, Aaron and Levi and all who fear the LORD to bless Him.',
    p: ['jacob', 'aaron'],
  },
  { // 136
    s: 'Give thanks, for His steadfast love endures forever.',
    m: 'Every verse of this psalm ends with the refrain, "for his steadfast love endures forever." It thanks God for His goodness, His creation of the heavens and earth, and His rescue of Israel from Egypt through the Red Sea. It also thanks Him for leading them through the wilderness, giving them the land, and remembering them in their low estate.',
    d: 'This psalm, called the "Great Hallel," was likely sung responsively, with the congregation answering each line, "for his steadfast love endures forever." It begins, "Give thanks to the LORD, for he is good," and praises the God of gods and Lord of lords who alone does great wonders. It moves through creation, how He made the heavens by understanding, spread out the earth, and made the sun to rule the day and the moon and stars the night, and then through redemption, how He struck the firstborn of Egypt, brought Israel out with a strong hand, divided the Red Sea, overthrew Pharaoh, led His people through the wilderness, and gave them the land of mighty kings as a heritage. It ends with God\'s present care: "It is he who remembered us in our low estate... and rescued us from our foes... he who gives food to all flesh," closing, "Give thanks to the God of heaven, for his steadfast love endures forever."',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 137
    s: 'By the rivers of Babylon, the exiles weep for Zion.',
    m: 'The exiles sit by the rivers of Babylon and weep as they remember Zion, hanging up their harps. Their captors demand that they sing songs of Zion, but they cannot sing the LORD\'s song in a foreign land. They vow never to forget Jerusalem and cry out for justice against Edom and Babylon.',
    d: '"By the waters of Babylon, there we sat down and wept, when we remembered Zion," the psalm begins, as the exiles hang their lyres on the willows. Their captors and tormentors mock them, demanding, "Sing us one of the songs of Zion!" but they ask, "How shall we sing the LORD\'s song in a foreign land?" They vow, "If I forget you, O Jerusalem, let my right hand forget its skill!", preferring that their tongues stick to the roof of their mouths than to forget the city they love most. The psalm ends with a raw cry for justice, asking God to remember how Edom urged Jerusalem\'s destruction and declaring that the one who repays Babylon for what it has done will be blessed, an honest expression of grief and outrage brought to God rather than taken into their own hands.',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 138
    s: 'David thanks God with his whole heart for answering him.',
    m: 'David gives thanks to the LORD with his whole heart before the gods, bowing toward His holy temple. On the day he called, God answered him and strengthened his soul. Though the LORD is high, He regards the lowly, preserves David in trouble, and will fulfill His purpose for him.',
    d: '"I give you thanks, O LORD, with my whole heart; before the gods I sing your praise," David begins, bowing toward God\'s holy temple and giving thanks for His steadfast love and faithfulness, "for you have exalted above all things your name and your word." "On the day I called, you answered me; my strength of soul you increased." All the kings of the earth will give thanks when they hear the words of God\'s mouth and sing of His ways, "for great is the glory of the LORD. For though the LORD is high, he regards the lowly, but the haughty he knows from afar." Though he walks in the midst of trouble, God preserves his life, and David closes, "The LORD will fulfill his purpose for me; your steadfast love, O LORD, endures forever. Do not forsake the work of your hands."',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 139
    s: 'God knows me completely, is everywhere present, and made me wonderfully.',
    m: 'David marvels that God has searched him and knows everything about him, even his thoughts and words before he speaks. There is nowhere he can go to escape God\'s presence, not even in darkness. God formed him in his mother\'s womb, and he is fearfully and wonderfully made, so he asks God to search his heart and lead him in the way everlasting.',
    d: '"O LORD, you have searched me and known me!" David begins, marveling that God knows when he sits and rises, discerns his thoughts from afar, and knows every word before it is on his tongue, a knowledge too wonderful for him. "Where shall I go from your Spirit? Or where shall I flee from your presence?" he asks, for if he ascends to heaven or makes his bed in Sheol, God is there, and even the darkness is not dark to Him. "For you formed my inward parts; you knitted me together in my mother\'s womb. I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made," he says, adding that all his days were written in God\'s book before one of them came to be, and God\'s thoughts toward him are more numerous than the sand. After declaring his hatred for those who hate God, he ends with a humble prayer: "Search me, O God, and know my heart! Try me and know my thoughts! And see if there be any grievous way in me, and lead me in the way everlasting!"',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 140
    s: 'David asks God to guard him from violent and slanderous people.',
    m: 'David asks the LORD to deliver him from evil and violent men who plan evil and have tongues like serpents. They set traps and snares for his feet. He declares, "You are my God," trusting God to cover his head in battle and to maintain the cause of the afflicted.',
    d: '"Deliver me, O LORD, from evil men; preserve me from violent men, who plan evil things in their heart and stir up wars continually," David prays, describing tongues as sharp as a serpent\'s and venom of asps under their lips, a verse Paul quotes in Romans 3:13. The arrogant have hidden a trap, spread a net, and set snares for him beside the way. "I say to the LORD, You are my God," David declares, calling Him the strength of his salvation who has covered his head in the day of battle, and he asks God not to grant the desires of the wicked. He prays that the evil they intend will fall on their own heads and closes with confidence: "I know that the LORD will maintain the cause of the afflicted, and will execute justice for the needy," and the upright will dwell in His presence.',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 141
    s: 'David asks God to guard his mouth and heart from evil.',
    m: 'David asks God to hear him quickly and accept his prayer like incense and his lifted hands like the evening sacrifice. He prays for a guard over his mouth and that his heart not be drawn to evil or the delicacies of evildoers. He welcomes the rebuke of the righteous as kindness and keeps his eyes on the Lord as his refuge.',
    d: '"O LORD, I call upon you; hasten to me!" David begins, asking, "Let my prayer be counted as incense before you, and the lifting up of my hands as the evening sacrifice!" He prays, "Set a guard, O LORD, over my mouth; keep watch over the door of my lips! Do not let my heart incline to any evil," and not to share in the delicacies of those who work iniquity. "Let a righteous man strike me—it is a kindness; let him rebuke me—it is oil for my head," he says, welcoming correction while praying against the deeds of evildoers. "But my eyes are toward you, O GOD, my Lord; in you I seek refuge," he concludes, asking God to keep him from the traps of the wicked while he passes by safely.',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 142
    s: 'Alone in a cave, David pours out his complaint to God, his refuge.',
    m: 'Written when David was hiding in the cave, he cries aloud to the LORD and pours out his complaint. No one cares for him or takes notice, and there is no refuge for him among people. He says to the LORD, "You are my refuge, my portion in the land of the living," and asks God to bring him out of prison so he can give thanks.',
    d: 'Titled "A Maskil of David, when he was in the cave. A Prayer," this psalm finds David hiding from Saul, crying aloud to the LORD and pouring out his complaint and trouble before Him. "When my spirit faints within me, you know my way!" he says, noting that his enemies have hidden a trap in his path. He looks to the right and sees that no one takes notice of him, no refuge remains, and "no one cares for my soul." "I cry to you, O LORD; I say, \'You are my refuge, my portion in the land of the living,\'" he prays, asking deliverance from persecutors stronger than he, and closing, "Bring me out of prison, that I may give thanks to your name! The righteous will surround me, for you will deal bountifully with me."',
    p: ['david', 'saul'],
  },
  { // 143
    s: 'David asks God to hear him, not judge him, and teach him His will.',
    m: 'David asks God to answer him in His faithfulness and not enter into judgment with him, for no one living is righteous before God. Crushed by his enemy, he remembers God\'s works and stretches out his hands to Him like thirsty land. He asks God to let him hear His steadfast love in the morning, teach him to do His will, and lead him by His good Spirit.',
    d: 'In the last of the penitential psalms, David prays, "Hear my prayer, O LORD; give ear to my pleas for mercy! In your faithfulness answer me, in your righteousness!" and adds, "Enter not into judgment with your servant, for no one living is righteous before you," a truth Paul echoes in Romans 3:20. The enemy has crushed his life to the ground and made him sit in darkness like the long dead, so his spirit faints, yet he remembers the days of old, meditates on God\'s works, and stretches out his hands, his soul thirsting for God like a parched land. "Let me hear in the morning of your steadfast love, for in you I trust. Make me know the way I should go, for to you I lift up my soul," he prays. He asks God to deliver him, "Teach me to do your will, for you are my God! Let your good Spirit lead me on level ground!" and to preserve his life for His name\'s sake, because he is God\'s servant.',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 144
    s: 'David praises God his rock and prays for blessing on the nation.',
    m: 'David blesses the LORD, his rock, who trains his hands for war and is his steadfast love and fortress. He wonders that God takes notice of humans, who are like a breath, and asks God to come down and rescue him. He sings a new song and prays for a prosperous, peaceful nation, for blessed are the people whose God is the LORD.',
    d: '"Blessed be the LORD, my rock, who trains my hands for war, and my fingers for battle," David begins, calling God his steadfast love, fortress, stronghold, deliverer and shield, who subdues peoples under him. "O LORD, what is man that you regard him, or the son of man that you think of him? Man is like a breath; his days are like a passing shadow." He asks God to bow His heavens, come down, touch the mountains, flash lightning, and rescue him from many waters and from foreigners whose mouths speak lies. Promising to sing a new song, David prays for sons like well-nurtured plants, daughters like corner pillars, full barns, flocks increasing by thousands, and no breach in the walls or cry of distress in the streets, ending, "Blessed are the people whose God is the LORD!"',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 145
    s: 'David extols God the King, who is gracious and near to all who call.',
    m: 'In this acrostic, David promises to bless God\'s name every day and declares that His greatness is unsearchable. One generation will tell another of His mighty acts, for the LORD is gracious, merciful, slow to anger, and good to all. His kingdom is everlasting, He upholds all who fall, opens His hand to satisfy every living thing, and is near to all who call on Him in truth.',
    d: 'David\'s final psalm in the book begins, "I will extol you, my God and King, and bless your name forever and ever. Every day I will bless you," for "great is the LORD, and greatly to be praised, and his greatness is unsearchable." One generation will commend His works to another, speaking of His glorious splendor and awesome deeds, and declaring, "The LORD is gracious and merciful, slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love. The LORD is good to all, and his mercy is over all that he has made." All His works and saints will speak of the glory of His kingdom, an everlasting kingdom whose dominion endures through all generations, and "the LORD upholds all who are falling and raises up all who are bowed down." "The eyes of all look to you, and you give them their food in due season. You open your hand; you satisfy the desire of every living thing," and "the LORD is near to all who call on him, to all who call on him in truth," so David ends, "My mouth will speak the praise of the LORD, and let all flesh bless his holy name forever and ever."',
    p: ['david'],
  },
  { // 146
    s: 'Do not trust in princes; trust the LORD who helps the oppressed.',
    m: 'The psalmist resolves to praise the LORD as long as he lives. He warns not to put trust in princes, mere mortals who die and whose plans perish with them. Blessed is the one whose help is the God of Jacob, who made all things, keeps faith forever, executes justice for the oppressed, feeds the hungry, sets prisoners free, and reigns forever.',
    d: 'The first of the five closing "Hallelujah" psalms begins, "Praise the LORD! Praise the LORD, O my soul! I will praise the LORD as long as I live." It warns, "Put not your trust in princes, in a son of man, in whom there is no salvation," for when his breath departs he returns to the earth and his plans perish that very day. "Blessed is he whose help is the God of Jacob, whose hope is in the LORD his God, who made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that is in them, who keeps faith forever." The psalm lists His care: He executes justice for the oppressed, gives food to the hungry, sets prisoners free, opens the eyes of the blind, lifts up those bowed down, loves the righteous, watches over sojourners, and upholds the widow and fatherless, but brings the way of the wicked to ruin, and "the LORD will reign forever, your God, O Zion, to all generations."',
    p: ['jacob'],
  },
  { // 147
    s: 'Praise God who heals the brokenhearted and names the stars.',
    m: 'It is good and fitting to praise God, who builds up Jerusalem, gathers the outcasts, and heals the brokenhearted. He counts and names the stars, yet He delights in those who fear Him and hope in His steadfast love. He sends snow and rain, feeds the animals, and has given His word to Israel as to no other nation.',
    d: '"Praise the LORD! For it is good to sing praises to our God; for it is pleasant, and a song of praise is fitting," the psalm begins, praising God who builds up Jerusalem and gathers the outcasts of Israel. "He heals the brokenhearted and binds up their wounds. He determines the number of the stars; he gives to all of them their names," for His understanding is beyond measure, and He lifts up the humble and casts the wicked to the ground. He covers the heavens with clouds, prepares rain, makes grass grow on the hills, and gives the beasts and young ravens their food, yet "his delight is not in the strength of the horse," for "the LORD takes pleasure in those who fear him, in those who hope in his steadfast love." He strengthens Jerusalem\'s gates, sends His command and snow like wool, frost like ashes and hail, then melts them with His word and wind, and He declares His word to Jacob and His statutes to Israel, for "he has not dealt thus with any other nation."',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 148
    s: 'All creation in heaven and on earth, praise the LORD!',
    m: 'The psalm calls everything in the heavens to praise the LORD: angels, sun, moon, stars and the highest heavens, for He created them by His command. Then it calls everything on earth to praise Him: sea creatures, weather, mountains, trees, animals, kings, young and old. His name alone is exalted, and He has raised up a horn for His people Israel.',
    d: '"Praise the LORD! Praise the LORD from the heavens; praise him in the heights!" the psalm begins, calling all His angels and hosts, the sun, moon and shining stars, the highest heavens and the waters above the heavens to praise Him, "for he commanded and they were created." Then it turns to the earth: great sea creatures and all deeps, fire and hail, snow and mist, stormy wind fulfilling His word, mountains and hills, fruit trees and cedars, beasts and livestock, creeping things and flying birds. Kings of the earth and all peoples, princes and rulers, young men and women, old men and children together are called to praise the name of the LORD, "for his name alone is exalted; his majesty is above earth and heaven." He has raised up a horn for His people, praise for all His saints, for the people of Israel who are near to Him, and the psalm ends, "Praise the LORD!"',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 149
    s: 'Let God\'s people sing a new song and rejoice in their King.',
    m: 'The assembly of the godly is called to sing a new song to the LORD and rejoice in their Maker and King. They are to praise His name with dancing and music, for the LORD takes pleasure in His people and adorns the humble with salvation. The psalm also pictures the faithful carrying out God\'s judgment on the nations, an honor for all His saints.',
    d: '"Praise the LORD! Sing to the LORD a new song, his praise in the assembly of the godly!" the psalm begins, calling Israel to be glad in its Maker and the children of Zion to rejoice in their King. They are to praise His name with dancing and make melody with tambourine and lyre, "for the LORD takes pleasure in his people; he adorns the humble with salvation." The godly are called to exult in glory and sing for joy on their beds, with the high praises of God in their throats and two-edged swords in their hands. They will execute vengeance on the nations and bind kings with chains, carrying out "the judgment written," an honor for all His godly ones, which Christians read in light of the spiritual battle and final judgment that belong to Christ.',
    p: [],
  },
  { // 150
    s: 'Let everything that has breath praise the LORD!',
    m: 'The final psalm calls for praise to God in His sanctuary and in His mighty heavens. People should praise Him for His mighty deeds and His excellent greatness. With trumpet, harp, tambourine, strings, pipe and loud cymbals, everything that has breath is called to praise the LORD.',
    d: 'The book of Psalms ends with a grand doxology in which every line calls for praise: "Praise the LORD! Praise God in his sanctuary; praise him in his mighty heavens!" Worshipers are to praise Him for His mighty deeds and according to His excellent greatness. Every instrument is summoned, trumpet, lute and harp, tambourine and dance, strings and pipe, sounding cymbals and loud clashing cymbals. The final verse calls all creation to join in: "Let everything that has breath praise the LORD! Praise the LORD!"',
    p: [],
  },
];
